/*!

@page readme README
@brief Introduction into MoDELib2

@section Dependencies Installing MoDELib2 Dependencies 

In order to efficiently use MoDELib2, a text editor is necessary. One can use XCode, Visual Studios, TextEdit, Vim, etc. as detailed in Appendix A **(RENAME?)**. Details on the versions of needed software are shown in Appendix B. 
 
@subsection MacOS MacOS

For MacOS, it is first necessary to download XCode from the Mac App Store. Once this has been installed, the command lines tools will be necessary. A terminal can be opened by simply typing ”Terminal” into the spotlight search. Once XCode has been downloaded, to install the command line tools, simply type the following lines into the terminal 

\verbatim
$ xcode-select --install 
\endverbatim

A prompt will then appear to accept the download of the command line tools. Now, to manage the installa tion of other MoDELib2 dependencies, it is recommended to install MacPorts as a package manager over other package managers like homebrew. Install MacPorts from the website: https://www.macports.org/install.php. Under the ”Installing MacPorts” tab, please install MacPorts for your version of the Mac operating system. Now other packages can be installed using MacPorts with the following line in the comman shell: 

\verbatim
$ sudo port install $package name$ 
\endverbatim

For example, MoDELib2 features a modern CMake-based build system, as opposed to the Makefile-based build system of MoDELib1. To install cmake via MacPorts, simply type the following into the command shell, 

\verbatim
$ sudo port install cmake 
\endverbatim

Now all dependencies to use cmake for creating make files for MoDELib2 have been installed in the /opt/local directory. The /opt/local directory is the default location for installations with MacPorts, unless otherwise specified during the installation of MacPorts. 


@subsection mac_dependencies Installing Dependencies


### Installing MacPort Dependencies 


To compile dislocation dynamics programs without any issues, you need to have the necessary library installed on your system. This includes eigen, fftw, pybind11, boost, VTK as well as QT. With MacPorts eigen, fftw, and pybind can be easily installed as follows. Open a terminal window and to install eigen type in the terminal, 

\verbatim
$ sudo port install eigen3 
\endverbatim

for fftw type, 

\verbatim
$ sudo port install fftw-3 
\endverbatim


| 			|	 		|
| ---		        | ---	 		|
| @image html 1.1a.png  | @image html 1.1b.png  |
| (a)			| (b)	 		|

Figure 1.1: (a) Boost GitHub Page, (b) boost installation for OpenMP type 

\verbatim
$ sudo port install libomp 
\endverbatim

finally for pybind type, 

\verbatim
$ sudo port install pybind11 
\endverbatim

which should also install the necessary python dependencies. All dependencies will now be installed in the /opt/local/ folder of your MacOS system. 


### Boost


First visit the boost GitHub page: https://github.com/boostorg/cmake?tab=readme-ov-file. Scroll down and click the hyper-link shown in Fig. 1.1a. Next download the zipped file from Fig. 1.1b and open the zipped file from the downloads folder. Once downloaded open a terminal inside the boost directory and perform the following terminal commands, 

\verbatim
$ mkdir build 
$ cd build 
$ cmake .. 
$ sudo make install 
\endverbatim

Once the binaries have been installed, the boost directory is no longer needed. 


### SuiteSparse


SuiteSparse is a suite of sparse matrix algorithms authored or co-authored by Tim Davis, Texas A&M University. It accelerate the solution of sparse linear system of equations compared to built-in Eigen solvers. Download from https://github.com/DrTimothyAldenDavis/SuiteSparse. To install isse the following terminal commands: 

\verbatim
$ mkdir -p build && cd build 
$ cmake .. 
$ cmake --build . 
$ sudo cmake --install . 
\endverbatim


@subsection Linux Linux 


@subsection linux_dep Installing Dependencies 


This section details the installation of MoDELib2 using a Linux OS. It should be noted that the author is running Ubuntu 20.04.6 LTS, although other distros of the Linux OS can be utilized. It is assumed that the package manager utilized throughout the process is apt; however, any package manager such as dnf that offers the necessary dependencies should suffice. In the event that the above packages are not available on the utilized 


Linux distribution, it may be necessary to find which versions of specific packages are available to the user. To find available packages related to a *keyword*, the following line will list all available packages: 

\verbatim
$ apt search keyword 
\endverbatim

To download the necessary dependencies type in the terminal, for example: 

\verbatim
$ sudo apt install cmake 
\endverbatim

will install the cmake compiling package in the default directory as specified by your OS configuration. For standard Ubuntu Linux, the default directory is /usr/local. The same procedure can be used to download all of the dependencies of MoDELib. It is worth noting that the text editor Vim is also available on Linux OS, and the instructions provided in Sec. A are applicable to Linux users. For convenience, the following commands have been provided to install the MoDELib2 dependent libraries on Linux OS. 

\verbatim
$ sudo apt install eigen3 
$ sudo apt install fftw-3 
$ sudo apt install libomp 
$ sudo apt install python3 
$ sudo apt install pybind11 
\endverbatim

OpenGL is required for many visualization-based applications, and can be installed by passing the following arguments into the command line. 

\verbatim
$ sudo apt install libglew-dev libgl sdl 
\endverbatim

### Boost 


First visit the boost GitHub page: https://github.com/boostorg/cmake?tab=readme-ov-file. Scroll down and click the hyper-link shown in Fig. 1.1a. Next download the zipped file from Fig. 1.1b and open the zipped file from the downloads folder. Once downloaded open a terminal inside the boost directory and perform the following terminal commands, 

\verbatim
$ mkdir build 
$ cd build 
$ cmake .. 
$ sudo make install 
\endverbatim

Once the binaries have been installed, the boost directory is no longer needed. 


### SuiteSparse 


SuiteSparse is a suite of sparse matrix algorithms authored or co-authored by Tim Davis, Texas A&M University. It accelerate the solution of sparse linear system of equations compared to built-in Eigen solvers. Download from https://github.com/DrTimothyAldenDavis/SuiteSparse. To install isse the following terminal commands: 

\verbatim
$ mkdir -p build && cd build 
$ cmake .. 
